# Design: Reusable recall-bench exercise

## Problem
Run 1 compared raw-transcript search against the ohya MCP with a
hand-assembled harness. To measure whether ohya improves, the exercise
needs to be rerunnable and comparable across time, binaries, and
question-set drift.

## Approach
Self-contained run directories plus offline comparison. Each run
records a manifest (harness commit, ohya binary/version, hub session
count, canary seed, question hashes) and snapshots its questions.
compare reads only recorded state, never the hub, so month-old runs
stay comparable. Drift in expected counts is handled by recalibrate,
which rewrites them for a human-reviewed commit. The ohya arm defaults
to bin/ohya so "build candidate, rerun, compare" is the A/B loop:
same canaries, same questions, only the binary differs.

## Key Trade-offs
- compare is offline-only: it cannot re-verify evidence, it trusts
  the finished runs' meta.json verdicts. Accepted because regrade can
  re-score traces separately when grading rules change.
- preflight splits failures into waitable (hub counts at or below
  expectation, local files exact: ingest or the 10-minute settle gate
  has not caught up) and hard (collision, missing file, over-count).
  Only waitable gaps poll; anything else fails fast rather than
  burning API spend on a broken corpus.
- recalibrate refuses nonzero matches on negative markers: a collided
  negative needs a regenerated term, not a rewritten expectation.
  Markerless negatives are skipped with a note since there is nothing
  machine-checkable.
- Explanatory comments were stripped per commentcheck; the semantics
  of waitable/hard and the recalibrate protocol live here and in
  docs/reference.md instead.

